"epic commit" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		&lt;epic-commit&gt;
		<header>The &lt;epic-commit&gt; element: one commit that built a phase or fixed an item, its short sha linked.</>
	</>

	<text class="lede" paragraph>One commit that built a phase or fixed an item.</>
	<list bulleted>
		<item>It draws the commit's short sha (its first 7 characters), linked to GitHub, then the doc's sentence about it.</>
		<item>The first of a run also draws a "Commits:" heading.</>
	</>

	<!--
		no <ui-list class="spell-meta">:  its <ui-item>s take Spell UI's "item" style sheet name before the
		              epics pack's elements can, and live examples may draw unstyled (issue I9 of epic-components)
	-->
	<text class="meta" paragraph>
		For anyone reading or writing an epic page's markup, or changing this element.

		Its vocabulary (every name it uses): <link target="src-spell-epic-src-components-epic-commit-epiccommit-en-ts" link="/epic/src/components/epic-commit/EpicCommit.en.ts"><text title="epic/src/components/epic-commit/EpicCommit.en.ts" code>EpicCommit.en.ts</></>.

		Its component: <link target="src-spell-epic-src-components-epic-commit-epiccommit-tsx" link="/epic/src/components/epic-commit/EpicCommit.tsx"><text title="epic/src/components/epic-commit/EpicCommit.tsx" code>EpicCommit.tsx</></>, with its style sheet (<text code>.css</>) and tests beside it.
	</>

	<section label="1. Example">
		<icon slot="icon" name="eye"/>
		<text paragraph>Two real commits of this epic's P14.</>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>Commits are hidden until something shows them: here a <text code>div</> shows them, as an item's or a phase's git icon does (<text code>--epic-commits-display: block</>).</>
			<item>Outside an <text code>&lt;epic-page repo&gt;</>, the sha is plain code, not a link.</>
		</>
		<html>
			<ui-stack style="--epic-commits-display: block">
				<epic-commit sha="c56a42c6721933234e88a24b980679b70a85c894">details pages show recommended as a violet thumbs-up, no word (your comment on Q20)</>
				<epic-commit sha="f38e6060dafa3be79646bf56050f870da5e02072">the Plan changes box (chevron, a count, folded to start) and the Agents running panel (open to start)</>
			</>
		</>
		<text class="meta" paragraph>Live: the real element, drawn by the epics pack this page loads.</>
		<text paragraph>In the Log, the epic's change log (epic <text code>epic-engine</> P52): with its time (<text code>at</>) and a chip for each phase and item it names (<text code>refs</>). The Log shows them always, newest first; here a <text code>div</> shows them. A ref this page doesn't have is plain text.</>
		<html>
			<ui-stack style="--epic-commits-display: block">
				<epic-commit sha="f3f554068c0ffee0000000000000000000000000" at="2026-10-29T10:56-04:00" refs="p-choices-questions q55">an option set works anywhere and answers any way</>
				<epic-commit sha="e75a0e081c0ffee0000000000000000000000000" at="2026-10-29T11:46-04:00" refs="p-tests-inline">a manual test is answered where it is, in the epic page's Manual tests section</>
			</>
		</>
		<text class="meta" paragraph>The second commit's Plan changes box sat above the phases then; since 2026-10-11 (epic <text code>epic-workflow</> P6) each phase has its own, atop its body.</>
		<text paragraph>A long sentence (epic <text code>epic-engine</> J184): clamped to two lines, ending in "…". A click on its text, or Enter on it, unfolds it; another folds it. The chips come before the sentence, so a folded one still shows every one; the sha and the chips stay links.</>
		<html>
			<ui-stack style="--epic-commits-display: block; max-width: 420px">
				<epic-commit sha="9f402c0941c0ffee000000000000000000000000" at="2026-10-31T00:12-04:00" refs="p-commit-log">the Log draws every commit of the epic newest first, each with its time, its short sha and a chip for each phase and item its subject names, and a sentence this long folds to two lines, so the side bar shows the run of entries, not one entry's paragraph</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="2. Markup">
		<icon slot="icon" name="code"/>
		<text paragraph>From <link title="epics/epic-components/parts/p-more-components.html" target="src-epics-epic-components-parts-p-more-components-html" link="/epics/epic-components/parts/p-more-components.html"><text code>p-more-components.html</></>, the body of phase P14:</>
		<text paragraph>p14.html · two of its commits:</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<epic-commit sha="c56a42c6721933234e88a24b980679b70a85c894"
			  >details pages show recommended as a violet thumbs-up, no word (your comment on Q20)</epic-commit
			><epic-commit sha="f38e6060dafa3be79646bf56050f870da5e02072"
			  >the Plan changes box (chevron, a count, folded to start) and the Agents running panel (open to start)</epic-commit
			>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="3. Where it goes">
		<icon slot="icon" name="map"/>
		<text paragraph>
			<text bold>Its parents:</>
		</>
		<list bulleted>
			<item><link target="src-packages-epics-docs-epic-phase-html" link="/epic-phase"><text code>&lt;epic-phase&gt;</></>: its commits, oldest first, after Done (else Goal) and before Files</>
			<item><link target="src-packages-epics-docs-epic-item-html" link="/epic-item"><text code>&lt;epic-item&gt;</></>: the commits that fixed or built it, oldest first, last in the item</>
			<item><link target="src-packages-epics-docs-epic-section-html" link="/epic-section"><text code>&lt;epic-section kind="log"&gt;</></>: the Log, one per commit of the epic, with <text code>at</> and <text code>refs</>, among the review lines by time (epic <text code>epic-engine</> P52); drawn newest first</>
		</>
		<text paragraph>
			<text bold>Its children:</>
		</>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>prose: what it did, one or two sentences</>
		</>
		<text paragraph>Its link comes from its own <text code>repo</> (a child repo's commit), else the nearest <text code>&lt;epic-page repo&gt;</>: <text code>&lt;repo&gt;/commit/&lt;sha&gt;</>, opening in one shared GitHub tab.</>
	</>

