		<section label="Slots" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing open="false">
			<html>
				<ui-text paragraph>A <dfn class="spell-term" tabindex="0">slot</><ui-popup heading="slot" label="Where the element shows its own children:  the doc's prose, inside its drawing."></> is where the element's own children show.</>
			</>
			<table celled padding="compact" appearance="striped" unstackable>
				<row header>
					<cell>Slot</>
					<cell>Holds</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>(default)</>
					<cell>What it did, one or two sentences.</>
				</>
			</>
		</>

		<section label="Parts" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing open="false">
			<html>
				<ui-text paragraph>A <dfn class="spell-term" tabindex="0">part</><ui-popup heading="part" label="A named piece of the element's drawing, which a page's CSS can reach from outside:  epic-note::part(label)."></> is a piece of the drawing a page's CSS can reach.</>
			</>
			<table celled padding="compact" appearance="striped" unstackable>
				<row header>
					<cell>Part</>
					<cell>Is</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>base</>
					</>
					<cell>The commit: its heading (the first of a run only), then its line.</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>heading</>
					</>
					<cell><text code>Commits:</> with its icon, over the first of a run of commits.</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>line</>
					</>
					<cell>Its line: the time (with <text code>at</>), the sha, the chips, its sentence.</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>sentence</>
					</>
					<cell>Its sentence (its children): a long one is a button that unfolds it, or folds it to two lines.</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>time</>
					</>
					<cell>With <text code>at</>: its time, to the minute.</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>sha</>
					</>
					<cell>The short sha: a link when the page has a <text code>repo</>.</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>ref</>
					</>
					<cell>With <text code>refs</>: a chip per phase or item it names, after the sha, before its sentence.</>
				</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="5. How it looks">
		<icon slot="icon" name="palette"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>Hidden until shown: an item's or a phase's git icon shows its own, and the Log shows every one. They set <text code>--epic-commits-display</>. No page-wide git toggle since epic <text code>epic-engine</> P53: the Log lists every commit.</>
			<item>The sha: a small chip on the code-well background, in mono, the link colour. Without a <text code>repo</> it's plain code, in the text's ink.</>
			<item>The first of a run carries <text code>Commits:</> and a code-branch icon; the lines sit under the heading's text.</>
			<item>With <text code>at</> (a Log entry): a row like a log line's, the code-branch icon in its column, the time muted in mono, then the sha, the chips and its sentence; no heading, a hairline between entries.</>
			<item>A chip: a rounded outline in the link colour; one the page no longer has, muted.</>
			<item>A sentence longer than two lines (measured as its box changes size): clamped to two, ending in "…", the pointer over its text. A click on its text (not the sha or a chip, nor the end of a text selection) unfolds it, another folds it; the sentence is a button, so Enter or Space does the same. Unfolded ones are remembered per page in this browser, by sha (<text code>spell-commit-folds:&lt;path&gt;</>), so the same commit in the Log and under its phase folds as one (epic <text code>epic-engine</> J184).</>
			<item>No state colour; a short sentence doesn't fold.</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="6. Written by">
		<icon slot="icon" name="terminal"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item><text code>epic commit &lt;epic&gt;#&lt;id&gt; &lt;sha&gt; "&lt;sentence&gt;"</>: lists one, replacing the entry already there for that sha.</>
			<item><text code>epic commits &lt;epic&gt; --backfill</>: every phase and item commit in the doc's git history not listed yet, read from subjects (<text code>P3: Name -- summary</>, <text code>&lt;epic&gt; I3: ...</>). It reads the kit and every child repo, and files a phase commit by the phase's name, not its number alone: a phase renumbered since, or another epic's P3, is left out.</>
			<item>The backfill (<text code>commits --backfill</>, <text code>epic phase ... done</>, <text code>epic merge</>) also adds each commit to the Log, with its time and refs; it reads the kit, its child repos and its peers, merge commits left out. The migration <text code>log-commits</> filled every page's Log from its history once.</>
			<item>Both write <text code>&lt;epic-page repo&gt;</> from the checkout's <text code>origin</> remote, when it's on GitHub, and a child repo's commit's own <text code>repo</> from that repo's.</>
			<item>The converter turned the old phase and item commit lists into it (<text code>CardConverter</>, <text code>PhaseConverter</>).</>
		</>
	</>
	<html>
		<footer class="site-page-footer">
			<ui-divider></>
			<ui-text class="meta" paragraph>Last checked 2026-10-31 against branch <ui-text code>epic-engine</>.</>
		</>
	</>
